\subsection{Determinant, inverse matrix and invertible filters}\label{sec:theory-inverse}
\writer{TV7}

In Section~\ref{sec:theory-filters} we saw that a color filter changes the
color $p = [R; G; B]$ of each pixel into $p' = Mp$, where $M$ is a
$3 \times 3$ matrix. In Task 12 the project asks if we can get the original
colors back after the filter is applied. To answer this, we need the inverse
matrix and the determinant.

\textbf{Inverse matrix.} A square matrix $A$ is \emph{invertible} if there is
a matrix $A^{-1}$ such that $A A^{-1} = A^{-1} A = I$, where $I$ is the
identity matrix. If there is no such matrix, $A$ is \emph{singular}. If the
filter matrix $M$ is invertible, we can multiply $p' = Mp$ by $M^{-1}$ and get
$M^{-1} p' = p$. So $M^{-1}$ is the inverse filter: it brings every color back.

\textbf{When is a matrix invertible?} For an $n \times n$ matrix $A$, the
following statements are equivalent \cite{strang2016}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $A$ is invertible;
  \item $\det(A) \neq 0$;
  \item the rows (or the columns) of $A$ are linearly independent;
  \item $Ax = 0$ has only the solution $x = 0$.
\end{itemize}
For a filter, the last statement means that two different colors never give
the same result. If they did, we could not know which one was the original.

\textbf{How to compute.} For a $3 \times 3$ matrix, the determinant can be
found by expanding along the first row:
\[
\det \begin{pmatrix}
a & b & c \\
d & e & f \\
g & h & i
\end{pmatrix}
= a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg)
\]
The inverse is $A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(A)} C^T$, where $C$ is the matrix of
cofactors $C_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ is the determinant of the
$2 \times 2$ matrix left after deleting row $i$ and column $j$. We must divide
by $\det(A)$, so the inverse does not exist when $\det(A) = 0$. In our code we
use \texttt{np.linalg.inv}, which uses Gaussian elimination instead of this
formula because it is faster and more accurate \cite{higham2002}.

\textbf{Example by hand: \texttt{UserMatrix}.} The filter in Task 11 is
\[
U = \begin{pmatrix}
0.7 & 0.15 & 0.15 \\
0.15 & 0.7 & 0.15 \\
0.15 & 0.15 & 0.7
\end{pmatrix}
\]
The cofactors of the first row are
\[
C_{11} = 0.7 \cdot 0.7 - 0.15 \cdot 0.15 = 0.4675, \qquad
C_{12} = C_{13} = -(0.15 \cdot 0.7 - 0.15 \cdot 0.15) = -0.0825
\]
so
\[
\det(U) = 0.7 \cdot 0.4675 + 0.15 \cdot (-0.0825) + 0.15 \cdot (-0.0825)
= 0.32725 - 0.02475 = 0.3025 \neq 0
\]
The determinant is not zero, so $U$ is invertible. This is the same number
that our code prints in Task 12.

Because $U$ is symmetric and has the same number on the whole diagonal, all
cofactors on the diagonal are $0.4675$ and all the others are $-0.0825$.
Dividing them by $\det(U)$ gives
\[
\frac{0.4675}{0.3025} = \frac{17}{11} \approx 1.5455, \qquad
\frac{-0.0825}{0.3025} = -\frac{3}{11} \approx -0.2727
\]
Therefore
\[
U^{-1} = \frac{1}{11}
\begin{pmatrix}
17 & -3 & -3 \\
-3 & 17 & -3 \\
-3 & -3 & 17
\end{pmatrix}
\approx
\begin{pmatrix}
1.5455 & -0.2727 & -0.2727 \\
-0.2727 & 1.5455 & -0.2727 \\
-0.2727 & -0.2727 & 1.5455
\end{pmatrix}
\]
To check, the first row of $U$ times the first column of $U^{-1}$ is
$(0.7 \cdot 17 - 0.15 \cdot 3 - 0.15 \cdot 3)/11 = 11/11 = 1$. This matches
\texttt{InvUserMatrix} in our code.

\textbf{What the inverse filter does (Q5).} In each row of $U$ the numbers add
up to 1, so a gray pixel $(g, g, g)$ does not change. Other colors get 15\% from
the two other channels, so they move closer to gray and the image looks faded.
The rows of $U^{-1}$ also add up to 1, but the numbers outside the diagonal are
negative. So $U^{-1}$ takes away part of the other channels and moves colors
away from gray. When we apply it to \texttt{ImJPG\_User}, we get the original
image back. The difference from \texttt{ImJPG} is at most 1, because
\texttt{ImJPG\_User} was rounded to whole numbers. When we apply $U^{-1}$ to
the original \texttt{ImJPG}, the colors become stronger. Some values go below
0 or above 255 and are clipped, so this result cannot be undone exactly.

\textbf{Singular filters lose information.} If $\det(M) = 0$, there is a vector
$x \neq 0$ with $Mx = 0$, so the colors $p$ and $p + x$ give the same result.
Three filters in this project are singular:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{GrayMatrix} (all entries are $1/3$) keeps only the average
        $(R + G + B)/3$. For example, the two different colors $(255, 0, 0)$
        (red) and $(0, 255, 0)$ (green) both become $(85, 85, 85)$. Looking at
        the gray pixel, we cannot know if the original color was red or green.
  \item \texttt{RedMatrix} keeps only the red channel, so green and blue are
        lost. For example, $(100, 50, 200)$ and $(100, 0, 0)$ both become
        $(100, 0, 0)$.
  \item \texttt{DeleteGreenMatrix} (Task 8) keeps red and blue but deletes
        green. The colors $(200, 30, 50)$ and $(200, 220, 50)$ both become
        $(200, 0, 50)$, see Figure~\ref{fig:tv7-hinh1}.
\end{itemize}
For these matrices \texttt{np.linalg.inv} gives an error, and no method can
bring back the lost channel. Some matrices are invertible but very close to
singular, like \texttt{SepiaMatrix}; they are studied in
Section~\ref{sec:theory-cond}.

